    "budgetW": 500,
    "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/uisp/wired/es-48-500w",
    "note": "Published row: \"Total PoE available = 500W\". Per-port ceiling \"PoE+: 34.2W\" (802.3at Type 2, 50-57V); 24V passive PoE 17W per port on the same jacks, not additive. Max power consumption 64W excludes PoE output, so the published 500 W allocation - not the 500 W PSU - is what a PD may draw."
  },
  "notes": "Census from the vendor tech-spec table (https://techspecs.ui.com/uisp/wired/es-48-500w, HTTP 200, 583,292 B, server-rendered table, read 2026-11-20): Networking interface \"(48) GbE RJ45 ports / (2) 1/10G SFP+ ports / (2) 1G SFP ports\"; PoE interface \"(48) PoE/PoE+ 2-pair (Pins 1, 2+; 3, 6-) / (48) 24V DC passive PoE 2-pair\"; Management interface \"(1) RJ45 serial port, Ethernet In/Out-Band\". Before this pass the 48 copper ports carried no connector, no poeOut and no budget, so a 48-port PoE+ switch supplied nothing and `show power inline` refused the arithmetic. Also added here: the serial console (52 jacks modelled for 53 physical) and the connector on the two 1G SFP cages. Speed ladder: the page prints \"GbE RJ45\" with no 10M rung, so 1G/100M only; the SFP+ cages keep 10G/1G. Per-port ceiling added 2026-11-21, not inferred from the PoE class: the same tech-spec page prints \"Max. PoE wattage per port by PSE - PoE+: 34.2W / Passive PoE: 17W\" and \"Voltage range PoE mode - PoE+: 50-57V / Passive PoE: 20-27V\", and the quick-start guide (https://dl.ui.com/qsg/ES-48-500W_ES-48-750W/ES-48-500W_ES-48-750W_EN.html, HTTP 200, 24,302 B, read 2026-11-21) repeats both ceilings and states \"The PoE settings for ports 1-48 are set to PoE+ auto by default, so 802.3af/at devices can be plugged in and automatically receive PoE\". Before this pass the 48 copper sockets carried `poeOut: true`, a bare direction bit: engine/media.js `poeDraw()` bills it as 1 W and the faceplate's `num()` prints nothing, so on a chassis Ubiquiti rates at 34.2 W per socket a 30 W 802.3at AP was refused power on all 48 ports. 34.2 is the PoE+ PSE ceiling; the 17 W 24 V passive ceiling sits on the same jacks and is not additive, and neither number is the whole-box allocation. Speed ladder: the tech-spec row prints only \"(48) GbE RJ45 ports\", which is why these sockets ended at 100M - but the quick-start guide that covers both 48-port models (Model: ES-48-500W, ES-48-750W) states \"RJ45 (Ports 1 - 48) ... RJ45 ports support Power over Ethernet (PoE) and 10/100/1000 Ethernet connections\", so the ten-megabit rung is restored on all 48 and the 750 W sibling (whose own row prints \"10/100/1000 Mbps\") needs no such repair."
}
